{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3457","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3457","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3457\",\"parent_revision\":\"9c68da377b157fde05cd414ff494ed0d6c06767235097952ba9187ecfac4327a\",\"root_revision\":\"9c68da377b157fde05cd414ff494ed0d6c06767235097952ba9187ecfac4327a\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-09-06\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nA GitHub-backed work item carries two body representations: the raw human-owned `issue.body`, and an agent-owned authoritative body tracked via a Contents-API head record plus audit comment, where `backlog_groom` writes land (`gh_client.py:1722-1732`).\\n\\n`root_revision` digests `issue[\\\"body\\\"]`. So a human edit to the issue body moves root, the head record fails validation (`github_work_items.py:368`), and the resolver falls back to returning the raw human body.\\n\\nThat much is by design and is what makes human edits visible. The loss happens afterwards: on the next reconcile, `parse_issue_body` builds sections **only from the body present**, and `_candidate` takes `candidate = remote`. The groomed sections are dropped from the working set.\\n\\n## What a user sees\\n\\nNothing. The edit succeeds, the item reads correctly, and `backlog_view` returns the edited text. The groomed content disappears at a later, unrelated moment, and the mismatch that caused it is not surfaced at any point.\\n\\nRecovery is possible by hand from `dh-content`, which requires knowing that the loss occurred and where to look.\\n\\n## Reachable from ordinary use\\n\\n`gh issue edit` on a backlog-managed issue is the trigger. Nothing about that command, the issue, or the repo indicates that some issues carry a second representation and others do not. Eighteen issue bodies were edited this way in one session; none happened to be groomed, which was established afterwards by query rather than known beforehand.\\n\\n## Why a guard is the wrong shape\\n\\n`issue.body` is the human-owned representation by design. A check that blocks or reverts human edits contradicts the invariant the two-representation split exists to protect. The gap is that the mismatch is computed and then discarded \u2014 the branch that detects the failed head validation is where the condition is already known.\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n`ARCHITECTURE.md:835` forbids writing `issue.body` from the sync path for every item, not only groomed ones. That rule governs the code's own writes; it says nothing to a human or agent reaching for `gh issue edit`, which is the path that produces this loss.\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Session audit of backlog_core GitHub write records, 2026-09-06\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-09-06\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## Why A Guard Is The Wrong Shape\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`issue.body` is the human-owned representation by design. A check that blocks or reverts human edits contradicts the invariant the two-representation split exists to protect. The gap is that the mismatch is computed and then discarded \u2014 the branch that detects the failed head validation is where the condition is already known.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Reachable From Ordinary Use\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`gh issue edit` on a backlog-managed issue is the trigger. Nothing about that command, the issue, or the repo indicates that some issues carry a second representation and others do not. Eighteen issue bodies were edited this way in one session; none happened to be groomed, which was established afterwards by query rather than known beforehand.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n`ARCHITECTURE.md:835` forbids writing `issue.body` from the sync path for every item, not only groomed ones. That rule governs the code's own writes; it says nothing to a human or agent reaching for `gh issue edit`, which is the path that produces this loss.\\n</div>\\n\\n## What A User Sees\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nNothing. The edit succeeds, the item reads correctly, and `backlog_view` returns the edited text. The groomed content disappears at a later, unrelated moment, and the mismatch that caused it is not surfaced at any point.\\n\\nRecovery is possible by hand from `dh-content`, which requires knowing that the loss occurred and where to look.\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"534d65c4104c4ef529c5c3fbf288e28ab34b975e55ef6ce80104311dbe938144\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABTSAMig\"}"}